To study the effects of electron beam illumination, a $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ film spun on   
ZnO/ITO/glass was investigated using CL-SEM. CL-SEM measurements were recorded at $5\mathrm{kV}$ accelerating voltage and $2\mathrm{nA}$ beam current using a JEOL-JXA-8200 Superprobe SEM equipped with a cathodoluminescence attachment. The $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ film was exposed to a scanning electron probe $(5\mathrm{kV},2\mathrm{nA})$ ) at room temperature while collecting CL spectra at 5 s intervals. In CL-SEM, the 2 nA beam current was rastered over an area of $720\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ , yielding an electron dosage of $8.34~\mathrm{C/cm}^{2}$ over 30 s. During PSC device operation, a typical current density of ${\sim}20$ $\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ corresponds to an electron dosage of $600\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ over $30~\mathrm{s}$ , almost two orders of magnitude higher than the electron beam, so any beam-induced structural changes may well be mirrored during device operation.
The spectral time series shown in Figure 5 reveals a clear change in the emission characteristics of the film upon extended exposure to the electron beam. In particular, the longest wavelength emission initially centered at 767 nm quenches and shifts by ${\sim}10$ nm over the course of 255 s, whereas a lower wavelength emission emerges after approximately $30~\mathrm{s}$ , increases in intensity, and blue-shifts to $660\mathrm{nm}$ . A CL-STEM spectral time series shows similarly continuous changes in the relative positions and intensities of the high energy and bandgap emission peaks (Supporting Information, Figure S9). The continuous shifts in the peak
